JD9365TX MIPI-DSI panel driver (HT05S-30MI-A1, 5.08" 720x720, 4-lane)
for Raspberry Pi 5 / CM5 running Raspberry Pi OS (Bookworm or later).

Files
  panel-jadard-jd9365tx.c                 kernel module (DRM panel driver)
  vc4-kms-dsi-ht05s-30mi-a1-overlay.dts   device tree overlay for the panel
  Makefile, Kbuild                        build (Kbuild lists the module's objects)

Build outputs (in outputs/, with the intermediate files; git-ignored)
  outputs/panel-jadard-jd9365tx.ko        kernel module
  outputs/vc4-kms-dsi-ht05s-30mi-a1.dtbo  compiled overlay

`make export` copies the .ko and .dtbo next to the source, where they are
tracked by git (`make clean` leaves those copies alone).

The driver needs kernel 6.12 or newer (it uses `mipi_dsi_multi_context`).

There are two ways to build:
  A. natively on the Pi (simple, slower)
  B. cross-compile from WSL / Ubuntu on x86 (needs only the Pi's `uname -r`)

Both use the same targets:

```
make bootstrap  # once; runs bootstrap-wsl or bootstrap-rpi depending on
                # where it runs (from `uname -r`)
make            # module + overlay
make module   # module only
make overlay  # overlay only
make export     # build, then copy the .ko and .dtbo here (tracked by git)
make clean      # deletes outputs/
```

Finish with "Install on the Pi" below.


## A. Build on the Pi

```
make bootstrap    # once (same as make bootstrap-rpi here)
make
```

`bootstrap-rpi` upgrades the system, then installs the compiler, `dtc` and the
kernel headers meta package for the Pi's kernel flavour (e.g.
`linux-headers-rpi-2712`), which keeps the headers in step with future kernel
upgrades. If the upgrade installed a newer kernel, it tells you to reboot
before running `make`.


## B. Cross-compile from WSL (Ubuntu on x86)

```
make bootstrap PI_REL=6.18.50+rpt-rpi-2712    # <-- the Pi's `uname -r`
make
scp outputs/panel-jadard-jd9365tx.ko outputs/vc4-kms-dsi-ht05s-30mi-a1.dtbo pi@<pi-host>:~
```

`make bootstrap` in WSL runs `bootstrap-wsl`, which saves `PI_REL` in
`.pi-release` (git-ignored); later runs can leave `PI_REL` out. After that,
`make` cross-compiles for that kernel and checks that the module's vermagic
starts with `PI_REL`; without it, `make` builds for the running kernel as on
the Pi. When the Pi's kernel is updated, run `bootstrap-wsl` again with the
new `uname -r`. Running it again for the same kernel is safe and quick.

What `bootstrap-wsl` does:

1. Installs any missing tools (cross-compiler, `dtc`, `dpkg-dev`, ...) and
   creates `/rpi-build/` (asks for sudo only when needed).
2. Finds the Pi's kernel package in the Raspberry Pi OS archive.
3. Downloads the Pi's headers package (for its `.config`, `Module.symvers`
   and release string) and the kernel's exact source: the upstream tarball
   plus the Debian/Raspberry Pi patches (~150 MB).
4. Extracts and patches the source, then runs `modules_prepare` with the Pi's
   `.config` and release string (a few minutes the first time). The Pi's
   headers package can't be used directly on x86, since its build helpers are
   arm64 binaries, but no full kernel build is needed.
5. Checks that the prepared tree reports exactly `PI_REL`; the Pi rejects a
   module built for any other release string.

Everything goes in `/rpi-build/<uname -r>/` on the Linux filesystem, even if
this driver directory is on the Windows drive. Don't put it under `/mnt/c`:
extracting the kernel source (about 90,000 small files) takes about a minute
on the Linux filesystem but a very long time on `/mnt/c`, and Windows folders
are case-insensitive by default, which silently overwrites kernel files whose
names differ only in case (e.g. `xt_DSCP.h` / `xt_dscp.h`). Old kernels can be
deleted with `rm -rf /rpi-build/<old uname -r>`.

### If there are several builds of the same kernel

Some kernels have both a Raspberry Pi OS 13 "Trixie" build (`-1+rpt1`) and a
Bookworm build (`-1+rpt1~bookworm`). They use different compilers and have
different symbol CRCs, so a module built against the wrong one won't load.
`bootstrap-wsl` stops and lists them; add which OS the Pi runs:

```
make bootstrap PI_REL=... PI_OS=bookworm      # or PI_OS=trixie
make bootstrap PI_REL=... DEB_VER=6.12.75-1+rpt1~bookworm   # exact version
```

The Pi's `uname -v` names the exact version, e.g.
`... Debian 1:6.12.75-1+rpt1~bookworm (2025-...)`. If no package is found,
`PI_REL` is mistyped or the kernel isn't a Raspberry Pi OS package (e.g. a
self-built kernel), and this method won't work for it.


## Install on the Pi

Run from the directory holding the `.ko` and `.dtbo` (`outputs/` for A, `~` for B).

```
# Kernel module
sudo install -D -m 644 panel-jadard-jd9365tx.ko \
    /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/updates/panel-jadard-jd9365tx.ko
sudo depmod -a

# Overlay
sudo cp vc4-kms-dsi-ht05s-30mi-a1.dtbo /boot/firmware/overlays/
```

Add to `/boot/firmware/config.txt`, then reboot:

```
dtoverlay=vc4-kms-dsi-ht05s-30mi-a1
```

Verify after reboot:

```
lsmod | grep jd9365tx
dmesg | grep -i -e dsi -e jd9365
```


